<Layout title="Chinese Herbal Medicine and Neuroplasticity: Mechanisms, Evidence, and Limits">
    <header>
      <h1>Chinese Herbal Medicine and Neuroplasticity: Mechanisms, Evidence, and Limits</h1>
      <div class="metadata">
        <time datetime="2026-09-28">Sep 28, 2026</time>
        <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
        <span class="author">Shasta Fox</span>
      </div>
    </header><blockquote><strong>Author-submitted research essay.</strong> Educational material by Shasta Fox. Selected claims and identifiers have been checked; full citation verification and independent peer review remain pending. Interpretations are not established clinical guidance.</blockquote>

    <div>
      <section>
        <p class="lead-paragraph">Chinese herbal medicine is increasingly studied as a treatment for depression and cognitive decline, and recent reviews explain its reported effects through one mechanism: neuroplasticity, the brain's capacity to change its structure and function (Lv et al., 2024; Zeng et al., 2025). Research on Chinese herbal medicine for depression and cognitive impairment converges on restored neuroplasticity, especially brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) signaling and hippocampal neurogenesis, as its mechanism; however, that explanation rests almost entirely on rodent studies, and the reviews that advance it themselves call for clinical trials, so the herbs' therapeutic value remains a well-developed hypothesis rather than an established finding.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Neuroplasticity as a Unifying Explanation</h2>
        <p>The neuroplasticity hypothesis holds that depression involves functional and structural abnormalities in synaptic plasticity, in the generation and maturation of neurons, and in related neural circuits and signaling pathways (Zeng et al., 2025). BDNF sits at the center of this account. Hirshler and Doron (2017) treated BDNF as a biomarker of neuroplasticity and presumed that an herbal medicine raising BDNF in the hippocampus or prefrontal cortex acts at least partly through neuroplasticity. Sangiovanni et al. (2017) likewise reviewed botanicals as modulators of neuroplasticity with a focus on BDNF. Zheng and Zhou (2025) extended the framework to cognitive impairment, describing herbal medicines as regulating several pathways at once, including BDNF/TrkB, PI3K/Akt, Nrf2/HO-1, and NF-κB.</p>
        <p>This framework is useful, but it contains an inference that deserves attention: a rise in BDNF is consistent with a neuroplastic mechanism without proving that neuroplasticity produces the behavioral effect.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Evidence for Depression</h2>

        <h3>Animal Models of Chronic Stress</h3>
        <p>Hirshler and Doron (2017) screened preclinical studies and identified 30 traditional herbal medicines, comprising 8 single herbs, 15 bioactive constituents, and 7 formulas, whose antidepressant-like effects in chronic stress models were associated with reversing stress-induced losses of neuroplasticity. Sun et al. (2016) offer a representative example. Mice exposed to chronic mild stress received four Chinese herbal medicines, including <em>Radix Astragali</em> and <em>Corydalis yanhusuo</em>, orally for 14 days. The herbs lowered elevated corticosterone, restored the proliferation of hippocampal precursor cells, reduced hippocampal apoptosis, and produced antidepressant-like behavior in the forced swim test (Sun et al., 2016).</p>

        <h3>Herbal Formulas</h3>
        <p>Much of this research examines multi-herb formulas rather than single plants. Zhang et al. (2024) reviewed Xiaoyaosan, a formula used clinically for depression, and proposed that it acts by regulating hippocampal neuroplasticity. They also observed that no antidepressant therapy targeting hippocampal neuroplasticity has yet been established (Zhang et al., 2024). Lv et al. (2024) summarized both single active compounds and whole formulas and discussed the limitations of current research.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Evidence for Cognitive Impairment</h2>

        <h3>Aging and Single Compounds</h3>
        <p>Liu et al. (2006) gave catalpol, a compound from Rehmannia root, to aged rats and found increases in two presynaptic markers of plasticity, synaptophysin and GAP-43, along with higher hippocampal levels of protein kinase C and BDNF. The authors suggested that catalpol may reverse age-related loss of neuroplasticity (Liu et al., 2006). Cho et al. (2017) analyzed 33 papers on <em>Epimedii Herba</em>, a traditional remedy for "kidney yang deficiency," and proposed neuroplasticity as a framework for future research on its use in brain disease.</p>

        <h3>Stroke</h3>
        <p>Chi et al. (2023) reviewed how stroke damages synaptic plasticity and summarized Chinese herbal drugs whose active ingredients or extracts regulate it. They cited evidence that these drugs improve post-stroke cognitive impairment, but they also acknowledged that the specific mechanisms of plasticity-based strategies remain unclear.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Limits of the Evidence</h2>
        <p>Three limits run through this literature. First, the primary studies are rodent experiments; this essay does not assess controlled human trials directly. Second, most sources are narrative reviews written by researchers who work within the field, and at least one author discloses a patent on an herbal treatment for anxiety (Hirshler &amp; Doron, 2017). Third, the reviews themselves identify the gap. Zeng et al. (2025) called for integrated preclinical and clinical research, and Zheng and Zhou (2025) called for high-quality clinical trials to validate herbal medicine for cognitive impairment.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>The neuroplasticity hypothesis gives research on Chinese herbal medicine a coherent biological framework, and selected animal studies link specific herbs and formulas to higher BDNF, restored neurogenesis, and improved behavior. The preclinical sources discussed here do not establish clinically meaningful effects in people. Until controlled trials test specific, standardized preparations, the herbs' benefit for depression and cognitive impairment should be described as promising and plausible, not demonstrated.</p>
      </section>

